  <section id="code">
    <h2>Published code</h2>

    <Answer>
    <p>
      Three repositories are held here with their READMEs captured, and each is
      classified by what it asks the reader to supply rather than by who wrote it.
      Two accept no wallet material at all; the third reads a firmware source tree.
      Upstream files and the pull requests filed in response are listed separately,
      as source and patches rather than tools a reader runs. As of 2 August 2026 no
      malicious seed-checker was found in public code indexes, which is a dated
      observation and not an all-clear.
    </p>
    </Answer>

    <h3>How the repositories are classified</h3>

    <p>
      Each entry leads with the input its README asks for. That keeps a repository
      which accepts no wallet material distinct from one whose stated operation
      would require recovery material, without treating authorship or technical
      quality as a substitute for examining the interface. Vendor warnings about
      checker tools are preserved on the
      <a href="/response/scams/">scams and warnings page</a>.
    </p>

    <h3>Held in this archive</h3>

    <p>
      Three repositories are held here with their READMEs captured. All three were
      read before being described, and each was checked against the same question:
      what does it require the reader to supply.
      <Claim
        basis="verified"
        scope="The stated inputs, licences and described behaviour of the three repositories held here"
        source="Each repository's own published README, as captured, read before being described; behaviour is quoted from the repository rather than independently audited"
        href="/record/"
        captured="2 Aug 2026"
      />
      <Claim
        basis="derived"
        scope="The safety classification given to each of the three repositories, under one rule: a repository is classified by what its own README says the reader must supply, and by nothing else"
        source="This project's judgement about third-party proof-of-concept code, applied to the captured READMEs. No artefact establishes it, and it is not an audit of what the code does when it is run"
        href="/record/"
        captured="2 Aug 2026"
      />
    </p>

    <div class="repos">
      {held.map((e) => (
        <article class="repo">
          <div class="repo__head">
