    <h3>Noted without recommendation</h3>

    <p>
      One repository has the shape this catalogue is built to flag: its stated
      purpose is to accept dice input and produce wallet material, which is the
      input class that would make it worth examining closely. It is completely
      empty. There is
      no code, so there is nothing to examine and nothing to warn about beyond the
      fact that a stated purpose is not a published implementation. It is recorded
      so that its later appearance with content is a change rather than a
      discovery, and it is not linked, because linking an empty repository whose
      contents are unknown would be a recommendation this archive cannot support.
    </p>

    <p>
      Separately, forks of the proof-of-concept repositories were compared against
      their upstreams. As of 2 August 2026 none had diverged.
      <Claim
        basis="derived"
        scope="That the examined forks of the proof-of-concept repositories had not diverged from their upstreams as of 2 August 2026"
        source="Derived from comparing each fork against its upstream on that date. The comparison output is not published here, so this one cannot be rechecked from the archive, and forks created or modified afterwards are outside it"
        compact
      />
      A fork that adds an
      input field for recovery material to an otherwise safe reproduction is the
      realistic way this class of code turns hostile, so the comparison is
      recorded, along with its date, rather than left implicit.
    </p>

    <h3>The negative finding, and its limits</h3>

    <p>
      As of 5 August 2026, no malicious seed-checker was found in public code
      indexes.
      <Claim
        basis="unverified"
        scope="The absence of a malicious COLDCARD seed-checker in public code indexes as of 5 August 2026"
        source="A GitHub public-code search on that date surfaced none; the search is not exhaustive, and a tool distributed outside code hosting would not appear in it"
      />
      That is a time-bounded observation and not an all-clear: it says
      nothing about tools hosted outside public code indexes, which is where a
      working phishing checker could instead be distributed on a domain, behind
      an advertisement or in a message. The
      <a href="/response/scams/">scams and warnings page</a> records the vendor
      warnings about that vector.
    </p>

    <div class="ve-card ve-card--red">
      <div class="ve-card__label lbl-red">Why recovery material is outside this register</div>
      <p>
        Whether this incident affects you is answerable from the firmware version
        installed when the seed was generated and how that seed was created.
        Neither fact is secret. Any tool, repository or page that asks for a
        recovery phrase, a private key or an extended private key in order to
        answer that question is asking for material that cannot contribute to the
        answer. That is why this archive does not accept recovery material and
        why the input requested by each published tool is recorded explicitly.
        The corresponding vendor warnings are preserved on the
        <a href="/response/scams/">scams and warnings page</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
